"Quasi-λ-distance-balanced graphs (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0166218X17302202)\nciteturn1search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9.2 years ago; Crawled: last month; Author links open overlay panel Amirabbas Abedi ^{a}, Mehdi Alaeiyan ^{a}, Ademir Hujdurović ^{b} ^{c}, Klavdija Kutnar ^{b} ^{c} ... A graph G is said to be quasi-\\lambda -distance-balanced if for every pair of adjacent vertices u and v, the number of vertices that are closer to u than to v is \\lambda times bigger (or \\lambda times smaller) than the number of vertices that are closer to v than to u, for some positive rational number . ... In addition, quasi-\\lambda -distance-balanced Cartesian and lexicographic products of graphs are characterized.\n\nDate: 20 August 2017\n\nPages: 21-28\n\nVolume: Volume 227\n\n## Published by: Elsevier\n\n# Quasi-\\lambda -distance-balanced graphs\n\nAuthor links open overlay panel Amirabbas Abedi ^{a}, Mehdi Alaeiyan ^{a}, Ademir Hujdurović ^{b} ^{c}, Klavdija Kutnar ^{b} ^{c}\n\nShow more\n\nView PDF Download full issue\n\nCite\n\nAdd to Mendeley\n\nShare\n\n10.1016/j.dam.2017.04.030\n\n## Abstract\n\nA graph G is said to be quasi-\\lambda -distance-balanced if for every pair of adjacent vertices u and v, the number of vertices that are closer to u than to v is \\lambda times bigger (or \\lambda times smaller) than the number of vertices that are closer to v than to u, for some positive rational number . This paper introduces the concept of quasi-\\lambda -distance-balanced graphs, and gives some interesting examples and constructions. It is proved that every quasi-\\lambda -distance-balanced graph is triangle-free. It is also proved that the only quasi-\\lambda -distance-balanced graphs of diameter two are complete bipartite graphs. In addition, quasi-\\lambda -distance-balanced Cartesian and lexicographic products of graphs are characterized. Connections between symmetry properties of graphs and the metric property of being quasi-\\lambda -distance-balanced are investigated. Several open problems are posed.\n\n## Keywords\n\nDistance-balanced graphs\n\n;\n\nQuasi-\\lambda -distance-balanced graphs\n\n;\n\nTriangle-free graphs\n\n;\n\nBipartite graphs--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAffine Mendelsohn triple systems and the Eisenstein integers (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1002/jcd.21739)\nciteturn1search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6.4 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; # Affine Mendelsohn triple systems and the Eisenstein integers ... Nowak, ... We also prove that for any affine MTS, the qualities of being nonramified, pure, and self-orthogonal are equivalent. ... 2020; 27: 724–744. https://doi.org/10.1002/jcd.21739\n\nVolume 28, Issue 10 pp. 724-744\n\nRESEARCH ARTICLE\n\n# Affine Mendelsohn triple systems and the Eisenstein integers\n\nAlex W. Nowak, \n\nCorresponding Author\n\nAlex W. Nowak\n\n  * anowak@iastate.edu\n  * alexwn1212@gmail.com\n\n  * orcid.org/0000-0001-5914-914X\n\nDepartment of Mathematics, Iowa State University, Ames, Iowa\n\nCorrespondence Alex W. Nowak, Department of Mathematics, Iowa State University, Ames, IA 50011.\n\nEmail: anowak@iastate.edu and alexwn1212@gmail.com\n\nSearch for more papers by this author\n\nFirst published: 24 June 2020\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.1002/jcd.21739Digital Object Identifier (DOI)\n\nview metrics\n\nRead the full text\n\n  * References\n\n### Abstract\n\nWe define a Mendelsohn triple system (MTS) of order coprime with 3, and having multiplication affine over an abelian group, to be affine, nonramified. By exhibiting a one-to-one correspondence between isomorphism classes of affine MTS and those of modules over the Eisenstein integers, we solve the isomorphism problem for affine, nonramified MTS and enumerate these isomorphism classes (extending the work of Donovan, Griggs, McCourt, Opršal, and Stanovský). As a consequence, all entropic MTSs of order coprime with 3 and distributive MTS of order coprime with 3 are classified. Partial results on the isomorphism problem for affine MTS with order divisible by 3 are given, and a complete classification is conjectured. We also prove that for any affine MTS, the qualities of being nonramified, pure, and self-orthogonal are equivalent.\n\nOctober 2020\n\nPages 724-744\n\n### How to cite\n\nNowak AW. Affine Mendelsohn triple systems and the Eisenstein integers. J Combin Des. 2020; 27: 724–744. https://doi.org/10.1002/jcd.21739\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nON SOME PROPERTIES of QUASI-DISTANCE-BALANCED GRAPHS (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/322800453_ON_SOME_PROPERTIES_of_QUASI-DISTANCE-BALANCED_GRAPHS)\nciteturn1search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 months ago; Quasi-distance-balanced graphs, introduced by Abedi et al. in [1] generalize the concept ... distance-balanced lexicographic and Cartesian products are characterized, see [1, Theorem 1.4 ... From Theorem 1.5 in [7] if G is a connected quasi-λ-distance-balanced graph with δ(G) > 1 then it is 2-connected and only stars are the quasi-λ-distance-balanced graphs with bridge.\nthe family of generalized Petersen graphs was studied in [11, 14]. For more results on this and\n\nrelated concepts see [2, 4, 8, 12, 13].\n\nQuasi-distance-balanced graphs, introduced by Abedi et al. in [1] generalize the concept\n\nof distance-balanced graphs. A graph G is said to be quasi-distance-balanced if there exists a\n\npositive rational number λ > 1, such that for any edge uv of G, either |W u,v|=λ|W v ,u|or\n\n|W v,u|=λ|W u,v|. In this case we denote with QDB(G) = λ. In [1] it is proved that every\n\nquasi-distance-balanced graph is triangle-free and based on that result it is proved that the only\n\nquasi-distance-balanced graphs with diameter two are the complete bipartite graphs. Quasi-\n\ndistance-balanced lexicographic and Cartesian products are characterized, see [1, Theorem 1.4\n\nand Theorem 1.5]. All known examples of quasi-distance-balanced graphs are bipartite, so the\n\nfollowing question, which was posed in [1] is still open.\n\nProblem 1.1. [1, Problem 1.1] Does there exist a non-bipartite quasi-distance-balanced graph?\n\nSince a graph is bipartite if and only if it contains no odd cycle, Problem 1.1 naturally leads to\n\nthe investigation of the possible structure of cycles in quasi-distance-balanced graphs. Edges of a\n\nquasi-distance-balanced graph G can be naturally oriented in the following way, for two adjacent\n\nvertices u, v ∈V(G), we deﬁne u→v if and only if |W u,v|=λ|W v,u|(see Figure 1 for an example).\n\nLet Q(G) be the directed graph obtained in this way.\n\nLet C=v 1, . . . , v n be a cycle of length n in a quasi-DB graph G. Let C+be the set of indices\n\ni∈ {1, . . . , n}for which v i→v i+1, that is C+={i∈ {1, . . . , n} | v i→v i+1}(here we identify\n\nv n+1 with v 1 and v 0 with v n). Similarly, let C−={i∈ {1, . . . , n} | v i+1 →v i}(see Figure 2 for\n\nan example).\n\nThe main focus of this paper is the investigation of the cycle structure in quasi-distance-\n\nbalanced graphs. The main results related to this are the following.\n\n2\n\nTheorem 1.2. Let G be a quasi-DB graph and let C=v 1, .. . , v n be a cycle in G. Then\n\nX\n\ni∈C+\n\n|W v i,v i+1 |=X\n\ni∈C−\n\n|W v i+1,v i|.\n\nThe following result generalizes [1, Theorem 1.2] where it was proved that every quasi-DB\n\ngraph is triangle-free.\n\nTheorem 1.3. Let G be a quasi-DB graph and let C=v 1, . . . , v n be a cycle of length n in G.\n\nFor n = 1, this class of graphs contains the quasi-{\\lambda}-DB graphs recently introduced by Abedi et al. [Quasi-{\\lambda}-distance-balanced graphs, Discrete Appl. Math. 227 (2017) 21{28]. Moreover, we will take a look at the problems arisen by Abedi et al. Some problems and conjecture are involved.\n\nView\n\nShow abstract\n\n... In [2] it is shown that even order distance-balanced graphs are also equal to opportunity graphs. Finally, let